  "- [ ] `evolution/stabilization-decision` Establish an exact stable contract and compatibility baseline\n\nStatus: Open\n\nSource requirement: `governance/stabilization-decision`\n\nRequired outputs: Stabilization record naming contract/surfaces, evidence and accountable decision Future compatibility baseline, package/tooling metadata and lifecycle release notes Owned deprecation/removal decision and record naming contract/export/profile surfaces, replacement or explicit no-replacement rationale, diagnostic id, warning/error/removal schedule, migration artifacts, retained-until-removal tests and release/package visibility; security emergency links to GOV4 and GOV2.\n\nClosing evidence: Require conformance history, negative fixtures, compatibility analysis, documentation and migration readiness for the exact surfaces; safety/security/library changes include applicable evidence and GOV4/GOV3/STD20 review. Close or update GOV7 experiment records; accepted RFC/module admission alone does not confer stability. Deprecation and removal retain replacement/rationale, diagnostic/schedule/migration and tests until removal under GOV8. Security emergency removals require both GOV4 and GOV2 records; release tooling lists stabilized/deprecated/removed contracts and source-qualified consumer outputs. Actual complete accepted artifact and consumer result for the named source contract, with input/source/policy/tool identity. Historical stage0 records or a passing schema alone do not implement these outcomes. Produce a governed deprecation record with replacement or explicit no-replacement rationale, structured diagnostic, warning/error/removal schedule and migration artifacts. Keep deprecated contracts supported and test-covered until the approved removal window; removal proceeds after that window or an explicit GOV4/GOV2 security emergency. Compiler diagnostics, docs, package warnings/rejection, migration tools or structured fix data and release notes consume the exact record. For